From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 02:10:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: misc: h713: probe the HDMI-RX wrapper windows

0088 established that the HDMI-RX EDID port is dead -- DMA_CONFIG11 will not
take a write -- while HPD control in the same window is live, the global enable
sticks, and the block was never in reset. The remaining hypothesis is the H713
wrapper the peer RE port writes BEFORE any Synopsys register: a controller
block at 0x06800800 carrying port-select and a PHY reset bit, and a
byte-multiplexed per-port state block at 0x06840000. A per-port EDID RAM with
no port selected would read exactly as observed: uniformly zero, ignoring
writes.

Neither window is described by any DT node, so neither has a verified power
state, and an access to an unpowered block on this SoC hard-locks the board --
no abort, no watchdog, power cycle to recover. That has already happened once
this session.

So this probes them READ ONLY, from inside the driver with the TV domains held
up, one window at a time, with a log line naming each window immediately before
its first access. Three reasons that shape is worth a boot on its own:

  - A read settles the actual question. "Is this window alive?" does not need a
    write, and the write sequence is worthless if the read hangs.
  - A hang stays attributable. The last line printed names the window that did
    it, instead of leaving a silent board and a guess.
  - A write first would make a hang ambiguous between "unpowered" and "we
    reset something into a bad state".

RESULT: both windows are alive with the TV domains up. Nothing hung.

  rx-ctrl+0x0800..0x081c   all zero
  rx-wrap+0x0000 = 0xc0c0c0c0   +0x04 = 0xcececece   +0x08 = 0x01010101
  rx-wrap+0x001c = 0x18181818   +0x20 = 0x09090909

Every byte repeated four times across each word, which is what the peer's
"byte-multiplexed per-port state" looks like from a 32-bit read: four ports
side by side in one word. Exploring further from userspace -- possible now
because this driver holds the domains up -- the controller window has real
content exactly where the peer says it does:

  0x068008f4 = 0x80808080
  0x068008f8 = 0x08080808
  0x068008fc = 0x33333333    the documented PHY-reset control
  0x068008f0 = 0x00000000    port-select, and it is ZERO

So no port is selected, which is precisely the predicted cause of a per-port
EDID RAM reading uniformly zero and refusing writes.

Setting it did not work, and that is the next thread rather than a refutation:
a 32-bit write of 0x01010101 or 0x00000100 to 0x068008f0 reads back
0x00000000, while neighbouring registers in the same window plainly hold
values. The peer's driver touches this entire block with writeb() and never
writel(), so the live hypothesis is that these are byte-wide registers that
ignore word writes -- which mmio-rw, being 32-bit only, cannot test. That needs
a byte-access path before the question can be answered either way.

OUT OF SERIES. Applies after 0088.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c b/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
--- a/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
@@ -112,6 +112,57 @@
 	dev_info(tv->dev, "wrote %zu EDID bytes, released HPD\n", len);
 }
 
+/*
+ * H713 HDMI-RX wrapper windows, per the peer RE port: a controller block
+ * carrying port-select and a PHY reset bit, and a byte-multiplexed per-port
+ * state block. Neither is described by any DT node, so neither has a verified
+ * power state -- and an access to an unpowered block on this SoC hard-locks the
+ * board with no abort and no watchdog.
+ *
+ * READ ONLY, and one window at a time with a log line before each first touch.
+ * The question this answers is "is this window alive with the TV domains up?",
+ * which a read settles; a write sequence would be worthless if the read hangs,
+ * and would make a hang ambiguous between "unpowered" and "we broke it".
+ * If the board stops here, the last line printed names the window that did it.
+ */
+#define H713_RX_CTRL_BASE		0x06800000
+#define H713_RX_CTRL_SIZE		0x1000
+#define H713_RX_CTRL_PORTSEL		0x08F1	/* byte */
+#define H713_RX_WRAP_BASE		0x06840000
+#define H713_RX_WRAP_SIZE		0x1000
+
+static void h713_probe_window(struct h713_tvcap *tv, const char *what,
+			      phys_addr_t base, size_t size, u32 off, int words)
+{
+	void __iomem *p;
+	int i;
+
+	p = devm_ioremap(tv->dev, base, size);
+	if (!p) {
+		dev_err(tv->dev, "%s: ioremap of 0x%08llx failed\n",
+			what, (unsigned long long)base);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	dev_info(tv->dev, "%s: about to READ 0x%08llx -- if this is the last line, that window is dead\n",
+		 what, (unsigned long long)(base + off));
+
+	for (i = 0; i < words; i++)
+		dev_info(tv->dev, "  %s+0x%04x = 0x%08x\n",
+			 what, off + i * 4, readl(p + off + i * 4));
+
+	dev_info(tv->dev, "%s: readable\n", what);
+}
+
+static void h713_probe_wrapper(struct h713_tvcap *tv)
+{
+	/* Port-select lives at +0x8F1, so read the 0x800 neighbourhood. */
+	h713_probe_window(tv, "rx-ctrl", H713_RX_CTRL_BASE, H713_RX_CTRL_SIZE,
+			  0x800, 8);
+	h713_probe_window(tv, "rx-wrap", H713_RX_WRAP_BASE, H713_RX_WRAP_SIZE,
+			  0x000, 8);
+}
+
 static int h713_tvcap_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
@@ -178,6 +229,8 @@
 		 readl(tv->thdmirx + THDMIRX_SCDC_CONFIG),
 		 readl(tv->thdmirx + THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG11));
 
+	h713_probe_wrapper(tv);
+
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, tv);
 	return 0;
 
